<!--Post: 1839--><!--Time: 1102997499-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1839"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 13, 2004 - 8:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;O Death&#34; the traditional folk song/poem that BTK borrowed to write his &#34;Oh Death to Nancy&#34; poem in 1978 had a big revival a few years ago. In 2000 it appeared in the movie &#39;Oh Brother, Where Art Tho&#39; and in the movies subsequent CD soundtract. In February of 2002 the song &#34;O Death&#34; by Ralph Stanley from that soundtrack won the Grammy award for Best Male Country Performance. Stanley peformed the song live on the national broadcast of the Grammy Awards show.  <BR>The CD soundtrack sold something like 5 million copies in North America and the movie was a huge hit. With all this attention on the song &#34;O Death&#34; during those years BTK must have gotten some self satisfaction that he would choose a little-known folk poem to parody in his Nancy Fox murder poem that would go on to earn a Grammy award 24 years later.  <BR>You would think that someone who craves media attention as BTK does would have made some reference to that in his recent communique&#39;s. Either taking credit for himself for discovering something that would eventually be recognized as an award winning poem or claiming in his twisted mind that that the movie got the idea to record the original poem based on the attention he personally brought to it through his parody version.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1839-->
<!--Post: 2851--><!--Time: 1104192041-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2851"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->J<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->J<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 27, 2004 - 4:00 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I posted a new thread the other day in the Discussion section called &#34;Oh Death &amp; P.J.&#34;  &#40;Perhaps that thread should be moved here?&#41;  <BR> <BR>In it, I mentioned that that song was popular very long ago and would surely have reached more people as a SONG &#40;not as a poem&#41;.  I suspect BTK heard this song as a child.  It&#39;s said to be a fiddle/bango bluegrass song ...and if it&#39;s true his grandfather played fiddle, perhaps this is how he came to hear it.  &#40;I don&#39;t buy the P.J. Wyatt class idea.&#41; <BR> <BR>I&#39;m also curious about the &#34;Anna&#34; poem.  It was given to police &#40;and Anna&#41; in June, related to a supposed foiled murder plot that occurred on April 28th. The poem refers to &#34;new awakening season.&#34;   <BR> <BR>I can&#39;t imagine anyone in Kansas thinking late April is &#42;early&#42; spring.  I think this is another indication that his poem was lifted from something else - and not original. <BR> <BR>BTK is not only a pathetic excuse for a human being, sick-o murdering freak.  He&#39;s a plagiarizer, too.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2851-->
<!--Post: 2872--><!--Time: 1104214994-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2872"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 27, 2004 - 10:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree with the notion that some of BTKs poetry sounds translated from another language or else written bu someone whose native language was not english.  I see a lot of that in florida, which translates as &#34;the flowery one, &#34; which I have actually seen in people&#39;s divorce documnents etc<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2872-->
<!--Post: 2873--><!--Time: 1104215400-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2873"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 27, 2004 - 10:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here is TheMoodyBlue&#39;s translation retranslated into English via Babelfish <BR> <BR>It was perfect plan of the irregular pleasure;  so in negrilla in that nite of the means.  My hot internal sensation with the propensity;  of the new station that wakes up.  It warms up, wet with fear and internal éxtasis;  My pleasure of the tangle.  Like new grapevines at night;  Oh, Ana Porqué made not to appear Him?  Drop of the fear, fresh rain of the means;  Nakedness would roll under his.  In order to suspect to the high fever, that burns inside;  In that small world of longing, of fear, the éxtasis, and the desperation.  The game that we played, that we fall in the ears of the devil;  Means of the fantasy advanced, assemblies to the fury of the storm;  Then calm of the winter in the end.  Oh, Ana Porqué made not to appear Him?  Only, now in another duration;  I put with enrapture sweet;  clothes through most of the private thought.  Bed of the humid grass of the means;  clean before the sun;  enslaved with control;  hot wind that suspects the air;  rasgones of the spark of the light of the sun;  in the so deep and clear eyes.  I was only above again;  in the memory of the passage of mirrors;  and to ponder because &#39;;  For number eight they were not.  Oh, Ana Porqué made not to appear Him?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2873-->
<!--Post: 2991--><!--Time: 1104359095-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2991"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, December 29, 2004 - 2:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Juno/Dpvina, <BR> <BR>Using babblefish to translate anything is an exercise in futility. Although you can get the general meaning of the translation, you never, ever get an exact translation. Improper conjugations are the most glaring problem, but completely mis-translated words are what kills it the most. <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t know what TheMoodyBlues used to translate the song, but his translation not only is grammatically incorrect, but that along with incorrect word usage or improper word translation loses or changes the meaning or intent. This is a fatal error of lots of translation. Word for word translation is always a problem, especially across large cultural boundaries. Even with the largest spanish speaking populations in the world just south of our border, it is difficult to understand intent in writing, especially poetry if one simply learned Spanish from a book. Fifteen years of cultural immersion has taught me a lot along these lines. But even being completely fluent in Spanish I still have trouble at times fathoming the meaning, or tenor of poetic verse in Spanish, just as it is likely difficult for someone with English as a second language to immediately understand English verse.  <BR> <BR>I do not know if BTK&#39;s grammatical errors in his poetry are purposeful or if they are a product of being uneducated. What I do not think, is that they are a result of translation, though some do bear the hallmark of this as you noted. <BR> <BR>The problem is that the revamping in his first poem of a song based on a poem that is linked by title to another poem that is steeped in a long tradition of poetry about dialogue with death dating to the middle ages seems a bit deep for someone of limited education. Sean spoke of memory of mirrors in the second poem. This reference in the second poem he wrote to the titles of two other poems by the relatively obscure English poet whose Death and the Lady is, as far as I am concerned, in part a source of the chain of literary work that ends in Oh Death, is a bit odd for someone outside the folk music / literature field. It is also interesting that this poets work deal mainly with death and murder. And even though BTK misinterprets the meanings of the poems &#40;well who is to say really except the long dead Mary Coleridge herself&#41;, it indicates at very least a passing familiarity with a subject matter very unconventional to people outside the folk music / literature community. <BR> <BR>J, <BR>As opposed to what has been written here, the first recordings of Oh Death were made in the late 1920s by Dock Boggs. That would suggest that if BTK learned this song via family, then the family came from either rural western Virginia, West Virginia or northwestern North Carolina. <BR> <BR>A good question would be whether the P.J. Wyatt class covered the writings of Mary Elizabeth Coleridge. Coleridge&#39;s subject matter &#40;death and murder very commonly&#41; coupled with the reference to two of her other murder themed poems in the Anna poem makes me believe there is a connection there.  <BR> <BR>My personal opinion is that BTK was at one time a student of folk music / literature or college level student who took courses that brought him in contact with relatively minor poets of the late 1800s. That or he taught the subject matter and purposefully slaughtered the transposition.  <BR> <BR>I am still digging around, looking for something similiar to &#34;Anna&#34; in late 19th century poetry to confirm my suspicion.  <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2991-->
<!--Post: 3023--><!--Time: 1104374930-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3023"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jujuree<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jujuree<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, December 29, 2004 - 6:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi, Ron.  It&#39;s great that you may have found two poems BTK referenced for his &#34;Anna&#34; poem.  I attempted to search on the internet, but since I didn&#39;t really know what I was looking for as far as authors, time periods, etc., I came up with nothing. <BR> <BR>If you do find anything else, let us know.  That could very well be information the WPD would like to know, too.  As you mentioned, it&#39;s not like everyone&#39;s familiar with the writings of Mary Elizabeth Coleridge.  It sounds like BTK would have to have an interest in folk music beyond just having &#40;supposedly&#41; a relative who played the fiddle.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3023-->
<!--Post: 3056--><!--Time: 1104393585-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3056"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, December 29, 2004 - 11:59 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Jan. 31, 1978: A letter containing a poem written with a child&#39;s printing set on an index card arrived at the Wichita Eagle-Beacon. The poem, which was patterned after a &#34;Curly Locks&#34; nursery rhyme, referred to the Vian homicide. The writer also referred to a mysterious &#34;factor x.&#34; <BR> <BR>I am a little confused by this excerpt from the History page. What poem does this refer to? Curly Locks is very different from anything in the poetry section &#40;probably because it a nursery rhyme no doubt&#41;.  <BR> <BR>One interesting tidbit though. If he did indeed send a Curly Lock styled rhyme about the Vian homicide, it ties in to his originating not from Kansas but from somewhere east of it. Specifically the area surrounding the Cumberland Plateau, where Curly Locks was used as sort of a courtship type rhyme. Of course Curly Locks as a nursery rhyme was much more pervasive than simply the area surrounding the Cumberland Plateau, but it is one more piece in the particular puzzle I am working. <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3056-->
<!--Post: 3076--><!--Time: 1104427719-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3076"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 9:28 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I am aware of Babelfish&#39;s shortcomings. <BR> <BR>I thought it was interesting that it contained the word &#34;mirrors&#34; and not much else. Oh, yes, also the point that words that are apparently synonymous have varying connotations and word choice can be of utmost importance.  Add to that the fact that the English language has upwards of 800,000 words whereas French has less than 100,000. It goes without saying how important word choice is in poetry.....Not that I would really consider what&#39;s at hand to be poetry, but word choice is important.  In this case, I often wonder if he meant to use an entirly different word.  Mrs. Malaprop and all that. <BR> <BR>I suppose I should be less parenthetical in my posts. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
