<!--Post: 3409--><!--Time: 1104881170-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3409"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->J<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->J<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 04, 2005 - 3:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Chiefron: &#40;Cool name&#41; <BR>Yes, as I&#39;ve mentioned before, &#34;O Death&#34; &#40;often not spelled as &#34;Oh&#34;&#41; is a very old song.  Dock Boggs wasn&#39;t the first to record it and we know it was a popular song both in folk/bluegrass circles and as a black spiritual.   <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t think BTK&#39;s knowing the song would necessarily tell us where his family originated.  Just my opinion. <BR> <BR>By the way, I agree that using Babelfish is often an exercise in futility.  I also believe trying to give any additional meaning to what &#34;B.T.K.&#34; stands for &#40;beyond his own suggestion of &#34;Bind them. Torture them. Kill them.&#34; is even more a waste of time. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m a scientist and the main thing we learn in interpreting data is that the simplest answer is usually the right one. <BR> <BR>&#40;On the other hand, I&#39;ve started a new thread that discusses a potential source for BTK&#39;s wording of &#34;Bind them, etc., etc.&#34; under &#34;BTK &amp; Literature.&#34;  Since it&#39;s not about poetry, I did&#39;t put it here.&#41;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3409-->
<!--Post: 3410--><!--Time: 1104883695-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3410"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 04, 2005 - 4:08 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->J--it has interested me that he uses &#34;them&#34; rather than just Bind, Torture, Kill<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3410-->
<!--Post: 3415--><!--Time: 1104884426-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3415"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 04, 2005 - 4:20 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Chiefron said: <BR> <BR>&#34;One interesting tidbit though. If he did indeed send a Curly Lock styled rhyme about the Vian homicide, it ties in to his originating not from Kansas but from somewhere east of it. Specifically the area surrounding the Cumberland Plateau, where Curly Locks was used as sort of a courtship type rhyme. Of course Curly Locks as a nursery rhyme was much more pervasive than simply the area surrounding the Cumberland Plateau, but it is one more piece in the particular puzzle I am working.&#34; <BR> <BR>More likely he read GAMES magazine, which was relatively popular at the time. <BR> <BR>&#34;During the investigation into the letters, the contents of the poems were also regarded as clues. It was soon discovered that the Vian poem was patterned after a &#34;Curly Locks&#34; nursery rhyme that had only just appeared in Games, a puzzle magazine&#34; &#40;<a href="http://www.crimelibrary.com/serial_killers/unsolved/btk/busters_6.html" target=_top>http://www.crimelibrary.com/serial_killers/unsolve d/btk/busters_6.html</a>&#41;. <BR> <BR>You can go here for the full text of the poem, rather than the more common single stanza. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.ongoing-tales.com/SERIALS/oldtime/POETRY/curlylocks.html" target=_top>http://www.ongoing-tales.com/SERIALS/oldtime/POETR Y/curlylocks.html</a> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3415-->
<!--Post: 3483--><!--Time: 1104957932-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3483"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 05, 2005 - 12:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->J, <BR> <BR>I agree that the simplest answer tends to be the right one. However prior to Juno noting for me that GAMES magazine had at the time just published an edition with a Curly Locks rhyme in it, I was attempting establish an accumulation of pointers toward his, or his families geographic origin. That information would simply narrow down the suspect list, at least for me. <BR> <BR>Unfortunately it looks more and more, like it was a wild goose chase and I wasted a good deal of time. As for O Death, the oldest musical recording of it that I could find was by Dock Boggs, who learned the tune from an older friend. Not that it really matters, but if you know of an older recording let me know, even though I think it may be a wild goose chase I will still toy with trying to pin him down geographically. <BR> <BR>As a side note Chiefron simply refers to me being an active duty Chief in the Navy =&#41; <BR> <BR>Juno, <BR> <BR>Thanks for that, not sure how I missed the GAMES magazine tie in. <BR> <BR>So we have a guy who creates poems by transposition in 2 cases and produces another with no source yet identified, though very Edgar A. Poe-esque tone.  <BR> <BR>I am also thinking that his references to poem titles by other 19th century poets might well be coincidence as well. My search for the origin of the Anna poems structure has narrowed a bit. It is not, I think, a rip of a Poe peice, but very well could be a rip of a work of someone heavily influenced by Poe, perhaps a later 19th or early 20th century American poet.  <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3483-->
<!--Post: 3501--><!--Time: 1104969198-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3501"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 05, 2005 - 3:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I was thinking poe earlier today!  Wow! But Poe wrote in a more structured style, following strict rhyme schemes and meter.  <BR> <BR>I was reading some stuff somewhere today &#40;i really can&#39;t remember, maybe crime library updates?&#41; that was mentioning James Joyce.  I can see some of it, but really wonder if it is a coincidence.  I mean, whoever this was said BTK was not using words wrong, but is a veritable genius who creates his own words and double meanings.... <BR> <BR>Personally , I would say that&#39;s pushing it.  Otherwise the poems would be...better structured?  More original? I would not be surprised if Oh Anna... was not basically a rewrite as you suggest.  We just haven&#39;t found it yet.  For some reason, I keep thinking Emily Dickinson too, but her style was different as well. <BR> <BR>Oh anna has a romantic bent to it.  Pretty twinsted.  However, some of it seems forced and i think it is a copy. <BR> <BR>I wish we had more letters to go on rather than bits and pieces.  A lot could be gleaned if we had the full text of the letters.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3501-->
<!--Post: 3532--><!--Time: 1104980948-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3532"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->J<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->J<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 05, 2005 - 7:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Juno: <BR>Regarding his use of &#34;them&#34;...instead of just saying &#34;Bind. Torture. Kill.&#34; :  This also makes me wonder.  In fact, it&#39;s this very thing that made the hair on the back of my neck stand up when I was reading the other night.  As I mentioned in another thread &#40;literature, not poetry, per se&#41;, the novel &#34;Lord of the Flies&#34; has a very similar thing in it.  Throughout the book, when the stranded boys &#40;who gradually turn from sweet little English school boys to murderous savages&#41; stalk pigs, they chant in unison &#34;Kill her. Stab her. Bash her in.&#34;  Alternately, &#34;Kill the beast. Cut his throat. Spill his blood.&#34; <BR> <BR>You can see the similarities ......not just using the verb, but adding the subject, etc. <BR> <BR>So, I came to wonder if BTK was a fan of this book &#40;a VERY popular book, even in the 70s&#41;. <BR> <BR>My guess is he is NOT a genius.  If he were, he&#39;d write original poems and letters &#40;and his poetry wouldn&#39;t suck&#41;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3532-->
<!--Post: 3535--><!--Time: 1104982072-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3535"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 05, 2005 - 7:27 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Juno, <BR> <BR>James Joyce is a possibility. I have not read everything from Joyce so will have to take a look. If I am not mistaken, Joyce did a version of A dialogue between Death and a Lady. There are many versions of that poem by many different artists. I believe one of them contains the wording that Oh Death the song comes from in it, but I don&#39;t have my notes in front of me right now.  <BR> <BR>While most of Poe&#39;s work did have a great deal of structure like the Raven, some others lacked traditional structure. There is even a whole cult based around the structure or lack there of in his poetry. Some strange folks out there. <BR> <BR>Well back to the books, even though I doubt it will gain anything. <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3535-->
<!--Post: 3553--><!--Time: 1105001006-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3553"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Armchairpi<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Armchairpi<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 12:43 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The authors at Crime Library agree with the James Joycian analysis: <a href="http://www.crimelibrary.com/serial_killers/unsolved/btk/14.html?sect=4" target=_top>http://www.crimelibrary.com/serial_killers/unsolve d/btk/14.html?sect=4</a> <BR> <BR>Anyone looked up Thomas King&#39;s writings?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3553-->
<!--Post: 3556--><!--Time: 1105006885-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3556"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 2:21 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I am going to have to respectfully disagree with the Joyce-ian analysis.  <BR> <BR>Joyce is hard to read, but there is an internal coherence in his work that BTK can&#39;t touch. The crimelibrary writers are off-base on this. BTK&#39;s poetry is just poorly written, period. Possibly intentionally so. Joyce was experimenting with dialect, he was gracefully injecting myth into his plot structures. His work, like Eliot&#39;s, is so chock full of allusions mythic and artistic that they could make for a course of study all their own. I have always felt that BTK took enough english in college to realize that he liked Shakespeare, and perhaps poets like Matthew Arnold. Profiler Pat Brown at one time thought BTK had translated &#34;Oh Anna&#34; because to her it had foreign-language based phraseology, and she referenced the word propension with it&#39;s use by the Portuguese. She has even amended her profile to admit BTK could have just been a fan of old english lit. I&#39;m not endorsing Pat, by the way, just using her as an example of someone else who may have spotted after closer examination different roots for BTK&#39;s &#39;inspiration.&#39; I think he simply, perhaps intuitively or with a book in hand, cobbled things together in his writing, taking phrases or turns of phrase that resonated with him and adjusting them &#39;to his liking.&#39;  <BR> <BR>Try reading Matthew Arnold&#39;s &#34;Song of Callicles&#34; - a simple google search will find you an online copy. It has a number of distinct similarities in theme and image to &#34;Oh Anna&#34;. I&#39;m not going to outright say I think BTK used it - but I think it&#39;s a good argument for his influence being archaic english literature. I also disagree with the Joyce comparison on purely aesthetic grounds. Joyce was one of the great english-language writers who blew the doors off the use of language in the 20th century. He paved the way for the popularity of Thomas Wolfe &#40;Look Homeward Angel&#41;, and even Jack Kerouac, who cited Joyce on numerous occasions as a strong inspiration. <BR> <BR>I personally am not going to ever give BTK a lot of credit for erudition or originality. He&#39;s not dumb, this much is obvious, but I cannot see him being a literary scholar. There is too much in the information the police have released as well as profiles done of him in the 70&#39;s and now that indicate a mind that was probably more of a mathematical and engineering bent. Plenty of people can be equally intelligent in diverse fields - a professor from Johns Hopkins whose name escapes me at the moment was both a Physics genius and a renowned poet, may still be living...&#40;Hollander? Not sure.&#41; - but the available profiles and even the evidence indicate BTK is not.  <BR> <BR>That said, it&#39;s not a small intellectual feat to grasp Shakespeare enough to essay pastiches of it - but it&#39;s easier than sounding like Joyce. And in the end, I think too much literary analysis of BTK&#39;s writings is making a somewhat simple thing complicated. I think of him always in terms of what he&#39;s not saying, of what sits in between the lines. The poems are perhaps to him real expressions of something...but his using them to goad law enforcement is nothing but a taunt, and a  bit of a red herring. His veils are his words and his piecemeal returns of trophies. I decided a little while back that I wouldn&#39;t worry so much anymore about the genesis of the poem and I started wondering about the more pertinent question, to me; why&#39;d he even send them? What is he telling us? It&#39;s not that he&#39;s smart and sensitive. I don&#39;t think he&#39;d try that. I do think it&#39;s a display of intelligence. I do think it&#39;s a taunt, and a slap in the face to those chasing him and the victims, and potential victims. It is a theatrical way to instill more fear.  <BR> <BR>In short, spending a whole lot of time figuring out the influences takes away from the chief issue; what KIND of person does this? <BR> <BR>It&#39;s that old saw of criminal profiling; behavior reflects personality. BTK doesn&#39;t just write poems, he writes poems about people he killed and wanted to kill. One of them was a children&#39;s rhyme. That, for instance, makes me think of the playground, and children taunting each other with rhymes and songs. This is what I&#39;m saying. Analyze the poems by their purpose, not their structure. <BR> <BR>Mind you, it&#39;s important I close by saying I only arrived at these conclusions after I&#39;d done ALL of what I&#39;m saying now might not be the most important thing. I have spent at least one entire night on the internet, which is one too many, figuring out the morphology of BTK&#39;s poems, when my time probably was better spent wondering about his motivations. If any of my time spent on this could be called &#34;better spent.&#34;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3556-->
<!--Post: 3558--><!--Time: 1105015598-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3558"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 4:46 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve Huff--Well said.  I could not agree more.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3558-->
<!--Post: 3566--><!--Time: 1105030297-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3566"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Skov<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Skov<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 8:51 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Steve <BR> <BR>Great post, and I think you are absolutly right. <BR> <BR>The word that came into my mind while reading your post was DECADENT, meaning that I think that BTK is a person driven by, among other things, decadence.  <BR>I´m not realy sure what I want to say with this, I´m just sharing because the feeling was very strong. språk <BR>By the way my first language is Danish and swedish &#40;located in Denmark&#41; so please bear over with my spelling and wording. <BR> <BR>skov<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3566-->
<!--Post: 3571--><!--Time: 1105037159-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3571"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 10:45 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, <BR> <BR>I have to disagree to an extent. Why? I truly feel that understanding the origin and comparing what he changed and perhaps why, will lead to the motivation for them. <BR> <BR>I cannot simply assume that they are taunts and leave it at that. Understanding what he changed, leads to more sound assumptions as to why he changed them. These things lend us a better understanding of the person behind it and their motivations I think.  <BR> <BR>After a night of Joyce reading though, I will have to agree that I myself could not find any connection there. I admit I am a poetry fan and not scholar, but the &#34;analysis&#34; at crime library is more of a claim with no support than an analysis. Its like me saying that his poetry was heavily influenced by Coleridge. It sounds good, but there is no real proof that it is true and anything more than a cursory review of Joyce&#39;s work shows that. <BR> <BR>Originally I though I might be able to connect the dots of his work and narrow down a geographic origin of his family. That way I could work out a social profile of him rather than a psychological one. Unfortunately I don&#39;t think that plan will work out. However I have not abandoned it entirely. <BR> <BR>Steve,  <BR> <BR>Do you happen to know of any noted 19th or early 20th century poets who originated from the area between eastern Kansas and the Cumberland Plateau area aside from Ed Masters? I do not know of any, but had the thought that perhaps the origin of Anna might be found there. <BR> <BR>The longer I look at though, I wonder if Anna is not perhaps &#34;original&#34; in that it is sourced from many peices. I think that would fit the evolution of his work from nursery rhyme to &#34;original&#34; poetic work as he saw the art of his crimes evolving. But that just seems to be overthinking the problem. <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
