<!--Post: 3590--><!--Time: 1105049385-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3590"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 2:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;I have to disagree to an extent. Why? I truly feel that understanding the origin and comparing what he changed and perhaps why, will lead to the motivation for them.&#34; <BR> <BR>That&#39;s fair, Ron. I think I was a bit too definitive in my post about this issue, anyway, and expressing some self-directed frustration.  <BR> <BR>&#34;After a night of Joyce reading though, I will have to agree that I myself could not find any connection there.&#34; <BR> <BR>I&#39;m glad to hear it. And blessings on your head for putting yourself through a night of Joyce. I admire and understand his place in literary history, but find him frustrating reading. I felt like I was being a little ballsy perhaps in the eyes of some fellow posters by outright disputing the crimelibrary, but what you said is exactly right - they made a claim with little or no support. I said, by the way, that the analysis of Joyce&#39;s allusions to myth and the arts <b>could</b> make up entire courses of their own. Scratch that. This analysis <b><u>has</u></b> made up entire courses of study, I found. I found an online paper where someone went to the trouble to parse an entire chapter of Ulysses, for instance, breaking down it&#39;s individual references to things as diverse as Wagner&#39;s opera &#34;Tristan und Isolde&#34; &#40;an opera T.S. Eliot referenced as well, by the way&#41;, to, obviously, Homer&#39;s &#34;Ulysses&#34;.  <BR> <BR>But, crimelibrary - what is posted there forms a GREAT <i>basic</i> source of info for anyone researching BTK. But it&#39;s a little sloppy. I have a particular problem with some of the assertions made by the writer Rachel Bell in some of her updates, but to go into them would be a digression in this response. <BR> <BR>As to your idea about working out the geographic origin - well, I think it&#39;s possible...I personally think that based on BTK&#39;s accent, poetry influences - &#34;Curly Locks&#34; was a poem he parodied as well, and it was written by James Whitcomb Riley, Indiana&#39;s favorite native-born poet, and a poet who also wrote at least one railroading book, with a fellow named Bill Nye <i>&#40;a little before the Science Guy&#39;s time, though&#41;</i> - that he may have hailed from the Indiana/Illinois area originally...which is awfully broad, I know. Might be a start. I base the assertion on him possibly having known of Riley&#39;s work even prior to college - Riley was, much more than someone like, even, Carl Sandburg, a &#34;pop&#34; poet of his day, and Riley is still well-known to many average Hoosiers to this day.  <i>&#40;What little I can tell from the available recording of BTK&#39;s voice, btw, indicates a slightly flattened-out midwestern accent, one that pings my ear as being possibly Hoosier, particularly the northern/middle region of the state. In that case, by the way, you can call me Mr. ungrounded assertion. I&#39;m only working from being a professional vocalist and musician, and therefore extremely sensitive to nuances of voices, accents, and timbres. I do pride myself on having a knack for identifying people by voice alone, but that&#39;s not here or there. I think, too, by the way, that any &#34;distortion&#34; of BTK&#39;s voice in that recording was a combination of the bad quality of the original tape used and him speaking in a manner that doesn&#39;t match his natural vocal placement. He has what singers would call a &#34;swallowed&#34; sound. The voice phonates in the back of the throat, as if his tongue is slightly tightened in the rear. You hear it when he says the name, &#34;Nancy Fox&#34;...there&#39;s almost an &#34;aw&#34; sound in the open &#39;O&#39; in &#39;Fox&#39;, like &#39;Fawx&#39;. He speaks, from what little I could tell, like a man who at some point after puberty began speaking in a lower register than is natural for him. Serial killers are often &#39;inadequate&#39; males in everyday life, and this forced deepening of the voice matches up with insecurities native to this kind of psyche.&#41;</i>  <BR> <BR>I digressed after all - anyways - back to Riley. He&#39;s the kind of poet an Indiana kid in particular might have heard of early on in school. Illinois kids probably read a bit more Sandburg than average, and Pennsylvania children get even more than their average fill of Poe, as education from state-to-state in the past included more of that region&#39;s individual identity. Riley wrote poems that highly literate people might find a bit insipid - I won&#39;t assert I&#39;m highly literate, but I do find Riley&#39;s work a little insipid - and here&#39;s my point; BTK&#39;s tastes, as you are beginning to determine from what you wrote, aren&#39;t all that high-falutin&#39;. James Joyce is the purview of pointy-headed literary types the world over, and it&#39;s like I said - can&#39;t see BTK fitting in that groove. <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t know, Ron...I think that geographic profile is more a possibility than you thought. And I gotta tell you, it&#39;s intimately tied up with  the social profile. If any of us grow up in a certain region, we become to some degree creatures of that land. For instance, I can go live in Germany with a house tenor contract tomorrow, and I will still be a boy from Davidson County, Tennessee &#40;Nashville&#41;. I would NOT abandon this line of inquiry, if I were you. <BR> <BR>As to your question about other 19th/20th century poets originating in the area, I may have unintentionally answered that already, except I&#39;m not sure if Indiana qualifies as part of the Cumberland plateau. Does it? Also, were you referring to Edgar Lee Masters, Spoon River Anthology Author? Talk about interesting literary history, and writing...no place here, though, so I&#39;ll move on. <BR> <BR>Ron, I might be giving myself a big dose of the  insipid, but I&#39;m going to check out some Whitcomb-Riley with &#39;Oh Anna&#39; in mind. <BR> <BR>About your final point...I&#39;ve considered BTK&#39;s poems to only be derivative in one way or another for quite some time. Have you looked at &#39;Song of  Callicles&#39;, by Matthew Arnold? Open a browser and look at it side by side with &#34;Oh Anna&#34;, paying particular attention to theme. I saw enough to bother me. I do believe he either subconsciously or consciously put bits and pieces of remembered words together, again, editing them &#34;to his liking.&#34; I do not necessarily see your final point as &#39;overthinking&#39; at all. I would only point out that I doubt this &#39;evolution&#39; was intentionally done on his part, but is an indicator that he was also possibly continuing his education as the murders continued throughout the 70&#39;s. That would dovetail well with my own belief that he attended WSU in fits and starts throughout the decade, maybe just taking a single class here and there while working. I also believe he was a non-academic employee or relative of an employee there, and may have been able to attend with discounted or no tuition at all. <BR> <BR>In answering you I have discovered you are indeed correct that it&#39;s still a worthwhile pursuit to figure out the morphogenesis of BTK&#39;s bad poems.  One almost-last thing; I do still think they were sent as taunts - they are his way of saying to cops; &#34;I&#39;m smarter than you.&#34; They are meant to aggravate. But figuring out their sources is a worthwhile pursuit, after all. <BR> <BR>Thanks for the dialogue. <BR> <BR>Steve     <!--/Text--></td></tr>
