<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3595"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 2:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Skov; <BR> <BR>Don&#39;t sweat the language thing. I enjoy the fact that you are on here and interested, and putting a lot in...and frankly, I think we &#39;murricans <i>&#40;that&#39;s how we say &#39;Americans&#39; in the south :-&#41;&#41;</i>, don&#39;t often admit how nice it is to hear different perspectives. <BR> <BR>Like you latching onto the word &#39;decadent.&#39; To me that is a thought that is uniquely European in it&#39;s nature, and as it relates to BTK, absolutely correct. He is the evil side of the soul of the bourgeoisie, in the sense that I&#39;ve only heard that term used by Europeans. Look at the neighborhoods where he killed. All solidly middle class, quiet, uneventful places out there on the flat land in the heart of the nation. Then he writes these bad poems, possibly even basing some of them on bourgeois poets like James Whitcomb Riley. The decadence is literal. He is a hedonist,  possibly an anger-excitation type of serial, and is probably emblematic of a kind of middle class decadence. The &#34;decay&#34; part of the decadence is in the soul of middle-class America. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m going to post &#34;Song of Callicles&#34;, as it&#39;s public domain, for anyone who wants to compare it to &#34;Oh Anna&#34;. Not in the sense of thinking it&#39;s a direct copy, but that it smacks of something BTK may have familiarity with, something that could have even unconsciously informed his writing. I wll also post a couple of things about Riley, and if I find any poem that strikes me funny relating to this, I&#39;ll post a link or the whole thing. <BR> <BR>I said in the response to Ron that my profession is singing <i>&#40;opera, y&#39;all go ahead and groan&#41;,</i> but my first love was writing, and I&#39;ve studied poetry intensely since my early 20&#39;s, so I guess that&#39;s why this subject is of particular interest to me. I&#39;m fascinated by codes and ciphers, too, but we don&#39;t have any certified BTK codes to work with here, so I guess I&#39;ll have to wait on that.  <BR> <BR>I have a final, non-sequitur sort of thought to share about &#34;Oh Anna&#34; that I know I&#39;ve shared before, somewhere. BTK proves in the first word of the poem that he&#39;s no poetic scholar. If the poem can be linked to poetic forms, it&#39;s what&#39;s called an &#34;apostrophe&#34;, or &#34;...direct address of an absent or imaginary person or of a personified abstraction, especially as a digression in the course of a speech or composition..&#34; <i>&#40;thank you, dictionary.com&#41;.</i> <BR> <BR>A <i>literary scholar</i> would know that apostrophes typically begin with &#34;O&#34;. No &#34;h&#34;. That may seem like needless nit-picking, but I assure you it&#39;s not. A commonly studied poetic apostrophe, for example, is by that poet beloved of fictional serial killers, William Blake - the poem begins, &#34;O Rose, thou art sick...&#34;. Point being that if BTK based his poem directly on examples and was as exacting as the scholars I&#39;ve known, he wouldn&#39;t have made the stylistic mistake of putting the <i>interjection</i> &#34;Oh&#34; in the title.  <BR> <BR>I now formally promise myself, if no one else, that I&#39;m not going to ever get that picky about something again. <BR> <BR>Steve      <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3595-->
<!--Post: 3607--><!--Time: 1105056848-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3607"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 4:14 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Matthew Arnold &#40;1822-1888&#41; <BR><b>The Song of Callicles</b> <BR> <BR>Through the black, rushing smoke-bursts, <BR>Thick breaks the red flame. <BR>All Etna heaves fiercely <BR>Her forest-clothed frame. <BR> <BR>Not here, O Apollo! <BR>Are haunts meet for thee. <BR>But, where Helicon breaks down <BR>In cliff to the sea. <BR> <BR>Where the moon-silver&#39;d inlets <BR>Send far their light voice <BR>Up the still vale of Thisbe, <BR>O speed, and rejoice! <BR> <BR>On the sward at the cliff-top, <BR>Lie strewn the white flocks; <BR>On the cliff-side, the pigeons <BR>Roost deep in the rocks. <BR> <BR>In the moonlight the shepherds, <BR>Soft lull&#39;d by the rills, <BR>Lie wrapt in their blankets, <BR>Asleep on the hills. <BR> <BR>—What forms are these coming <BR>So white through the gloom? <BR>What garments out-glistening <BR>The gold-flower&#39;d broom? <BR> <BR>What sweet-breathing Presence <BR>Out-perfumes the thyme? <BR>What voices enrapture <BR>The night&#39;s balmy prime?— <BR> <BR>&#39;Tis Apollo comes leading <BR>His choir, The Nine. <BR>—The Leader is fairest, <BR>But all are divine. <BR> <BR>They are lost in the hollows. <BR>They stream up again. <BR>What seeks on this mountain <BR>The glorified train?— <BR> <BR>They bathe on this mountain, <BR>In the spring by their road. <BR>Then on to Olympus, <BR>Their endless abode. <BR> <BR>—Whose praise do they mention: <BR>Of what is it told?— <BR>What will be for ever. <BR>What was from of old. <BR> <BR>First hymn they the Father <BR>Of all things: and then, <BR>The rest of Immortals, <BR>The action of men. <BR> <BR>The Day in his hotness, <BR>The strife with the palm; <BR>The Night in her silence, <BR>The Stars in their calm. <BR> <BR><i>&#40;Some notes; the portions that remind me of &#34;Oh Anna&#34; include the final stanza, which reminds me of: <BR> <BR></i>&#34;... Fantasy spring forth, mounts, to storm fury, then winter clam <i>&#40;sic&#41;</i> at <BR>the end...&#34;<i> <BR> <BR>...From BTK&#39;s poem - btw, I think he simply made a typo on &#34;calm&#34;, and there is no special significance to the word otherwise - then... <BR> <BR></i>&#34;... Alone, now in another time span I lay with sweet enrapture garments <BR>across most private thought <BR>Bed of Spring moist grass, clean before the sun, enslaved with <BR>control, warm wind scenting the air, sun light sparkle tears <BR>in eyes so deep and clear...&#34;<i> <BR> <BR>...reminds me of this from &#34;Callicles&#34;; <BR> <BR></i>&#34;...—What forms are these coming <BR>So white through the gloom? <BR>What garments out-glistening <BR>The gold-flower&#39;d broom? <BR> <BR>What sweet-breathing Presence <BR>Out-perfumes the thyme? <BR>What voices enrapture <BR>The night&#39;s balmy prime?—&#34;<i> <BR> <BR>Any other thoughts on this? I&#39;m not going to take it personally if you disagree, but I would appreciate an explanation if you do, for my own enlightenment&#39;s sake. <BR> <BR>I also didn&#39;t want anyone to think I was confused on Arnold&#39;s dates, as I&#39;d mentioned him in the same breath with Shakespeare, previously - I did know he was a much later poet than Shakespeare, but wasn&#39;t sure how early in the 19th century he &#39;flourished&#39; ~ Steve&#41;</i><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3607-->
<!--Post: 3642--><!--Time: 1105078380-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3642"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->J<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->J<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 10:13 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, <BR>You nit pick all you want.  Your posts are very compelling.  This whole thread has become one of my favorites on the board. <BR> <BR>A note: BTK&#39;s use of &#34;Oh&#34; rather than &#34;O&#34; is another reason I feel he may have been exposed to the SONG instead of the POEM.  We&#39;ve all noted he&#39;s a poor speller, but it just seems to me that if he&#39;d studied a written version of &#34;O Death&#34;...he would similarly use &#34;O.&#34;    <BR> <BR>However, if instead of READING a poem, he frequently HEARD the song &#40;which I&#39;m predicting he did through childhood&#41;, he would likely assume the word was spelled &#34;Oh&#34; because, in my view, he&#39;s not a well-educated guy.   <BR> <BR>Having said that, your Callicles poem really has me thinking.  You&#39;re right that this poem has a similar feel to &#34;Oh Anna.&#34;  I&#39;m particularly struck by the use of the words &#34;garments&#34; and &#34;enrapture&#34; in both poems.  These are not your everyday words. <BR> <BR>&#42;IF&#42; &#40;big if&#41; he was influenced by the Arnold poem to write &#34;Oh Anna,&#34; it does not appear he had recent access to it &#40;his is so dissimilar to the original&#41;.   <BR> <BR>I agree that looking too long at the original sources to try to understand BTK is likely a waste of time. As you say, it may be better to ask why he sent poems at all and what kind of person sends poems about people they kill/want to kill?  Looking at the intended function of the poems is indeed an important issue.  BUT, we shouldn&#39;t lose sight of the fact that he &#40;probably&#41; didn&#39;t write original poetry.  Can this fact alone be a telling point about a killer&#39;s profile?  Is it something as simple as his interest in poetry but lack of talent?  Is there any reason to think it&#39;s more than that? I&#39;m not challenging anyone&#39;s idea here; I&#39;m just asking. <BR> <BR>Re the Curly Locks-styled poem and Riley:  See Juno&#39;s message above, regarding a Curly Locks rhyme appearing in a GAMES puzzle book just a few weeks earlier.  I suspect he was exposed to this kind of poem via a common source like that &#40;and we know he likes simple-minded puzzles like word find&#41;. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3642-->
<!--Post: 3665--><!--Time: 1105116599-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3665"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 8:49 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Will post more later but on the subject of the words enrapture and garment in both. The comparison is interesting. However I think BTK meant to write enwrapped rather than enrapture. It fits the context of that particular line a bit better. His earlier use of the word rapture in the poem probably drove that mistake. It is similar to my bad habit of putting a &#34;g&#34; at the end of words that end with &#34;in&#34; such as within. It is a pretty common problem, I looked it up once but cannot remember what it is called. My example is sort of a learned version of the mistake, his was probably more like incidental version. <BR> <BR>Will post later tonight with thoughts on his voice. I have what people call a sympathetic accent, I pick up quickly on how others speak and without consciously trying fairly accurately mimic their accent/inflection etc. Will listen to the tape some more and post again. <BR> <BR>Going off Steve&#39;s thoughts will investigate the Indiana vs. Blue Grass influence. I am not entirely certain that a child of the late 30s early 40s would have been exposed to much BG in Indiana, but I have been wrong before =&#41;. Will look closer at that today and tonight when I have time. <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3665-->
<!--Post: 3667--><!--Time: 1105118269-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3667"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 9:17 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;I am not entirely certain that a child of the late 30s early 40s would have been exposed to much BG in Indiana, but I have been wrong before =&#41;.&#34; <BR> <BR>I&#39;m not so sure of that myself, but I lived there for a while and I discovered a few things about the state that surprised me.  <BR> <BR>For instance, it probably has the largest population of KKK members in the country. Southern Indiana is, in many spots, surprisingly, and proudly, redneck for lack of a better word.  <BR> <BR>The education system is pretty good compared to southern states, but aside from Indianapolis and the ultra-northern and scarily crime-ridden Gary, Indiana is very rural. The southern region also shares a lot of identity with it&#39;s southern neighbor, Kentucky - the bluegrass state. In the Louisville area you find so many things with the cobbled-together word &#34;Kentuckiana&#34; on them. <BR> <BR>That said, it was definitely a shot in the dark. I&#39;m more secure in what I say about how the timbre and cadence of his voice struck me. The cadence was non-rhythmic, mechanical. For instance &#40;again&#41;, the way the voice drops and then &#39;flats&#39; on &#34;Fawx&#34;. Sounded almost like what we&#39;d think was synthesized speech today. The depth too...couldn&#39;t help but thinking it was &#40;maybe consciously, for disguise&#41;, lowered from what his conversational tone should, or would be.  <BR> <BR>As to the enrapture vs. enwrapped...really could be, I have no idea, because here&#39;s something that has also occurred to me several times; some serials suffer from what one writer termed &#34;semantic aphasia&#34;. Because they are often psychopaths, words do not have the same impact and meaning to them as they do to the rest of society.  This can lead to malapropisms &#40;Gacy apparently was full of these&#41;, and language difficulties in general. This possibility has often informed the way I view his known correspondence. <BR> <BR>Then again, he could just be a bad typist and too cheap to buy correcting tape or fluid. <BR> <BR>Steve<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3667-->
<!--Post: 3711--><!--Time: 1105149308-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3711"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsbarn<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsbarn<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 5:55 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Not to knock anyone&#39;s attempt to match BTK&#39;s style with master poets in the Oh Anna poem, but to me, if I had to compare the poem to anything else it would be something by Gordon Lightfoot. The difference would be that while Gordon Lightfoot wrote songs about obsessive romantic love, BTK&#39;s poetry involves a different sort of love.  <BR> <BR>To truly understand the &#34;spring moist grass&#34; where BTK treads involves a unique sort of empathy &#40;not the same thing as agreement&#41;. John Douglas seems more adept than anyone I have ever read about in doing this. I feel this makes BTK truly unique than previously studied SKs--he seems to have the same sort of feelings experienced by most with a potential true love with a potential strangled victim, if I am reading his poem correctly. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3711-->
<!--Post: 3723--><!--Time: 1105160235-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3723"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 8:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve_huff: I couldn&#39;t agree more with your, and several others take that BTK is derivative and usually seems to rely on someone else to provide the meter. Like Tom, I too know very little about poetry and it&#39;s not a primary interest of mine. But, when I see crimelibrary liken BTK&#39;s words to Joyce and others of that stature, it makes me want to puke. In my opinion, this is <b>exactly</b> what BTK craves; BTK is a nothing and  being compared with any number of great writers would make him ecstatic. &#40;Not a big reader of Joyce myself, he&#39;s too difficult for me and I don&#39;t mind saying so.&#41; If BTK can read and fully comprehend the interior monologue, wit, irony and extremely esoteric imagery of <i>Finnegan&#39;s Wake</i> and then churn out abstruse, muliti-layered poems evocative of this novel &#40;and by extension all writing of this caliber&#41;, then BTK <i>is</i> a genius and God help us all.  <BR> <BR>I like the Gordon Lightfoot mention although perhaps in a different and more pragmatic way: If one provided BTK with the music, he might be an adequate pop song lyricist. But since poetry isn&#39;t my thing, let me ask: Could those who appreciate poetry be a little vulgar and use a scale of one to ten &#40;one is the worst, ten the best&#41; and rate, from what you have read of BTK&#39;s poetry, the overall talent of BTK as poet? You&#39;d be doing me -- and maybe many others -- a great favor. I realize you&#39;d only be expressing your opinions. As I&#39;m largely indifferent to most poetry, I won&#39;t even bother to vote, but I hope others who enjoy poetry might. Thanks a bunch! And hey, if you think the guy&#39;s really gifted, give him a &#34;10.&#34; I&#39;m interested in opinions. Thanks again. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3723-->
<!--Post: 3733--><!--Time: 1105163161-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3733"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 9:46 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Why do so many people seem to want to turn this freak into a artist... then what?... A tortured artist that killed for his art... please... this guy is a sick murdering scum bag and needs to be squashed.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3733-->
<!--Post: 3743--><!--Time: 1105166044-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3743"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 10:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George; <BR> <BR>My ranking of his work is a negative 10. He&#39;s the William Hung of poets, even among psycho/sociopathic killers. No kidding <i>&#40;well, that William Hung part was a joke, but you know what I mean&#41;</i>. He&#39;s no writer. No, he&#39;s not an idiot, but he&#39;s no scholar or writer, and like any good parasite, who can only get it&#39;s sustenance by mindless drawing the essence out of a host, he couldn&#39;t create his own poem if it saved his life. Everything he did was a tortured or twisted version of something else, or a patchwork of influences. He is a &#34;Hollow Man.&#34; But I don&#39;t think Mr. Eliot had someone as evil as BTK in mind when he wrote about his own Hollow Men. <BR> <BR>The point in picking apart the poetry is never, to me, to talk about it&#39;s merit: it has none, artistically. It is to figure out his what, where, why, and <b>who.</b> Figuring out what poets he stole from gives us some sociological info, maybe, and as Chiefron is thinking, even geographical info - at least a hint. Noting the way he patterns his writing - use of noun, verb, adjective, - that&#39;s forensic linguistics, and could help tie in a suspect with the letters and poems if we ever get that far. Determining the intent behind the poem - other than his obvious desire to taunt the public and law enforcement - will tell us the way he sees the world.  <BR> <BR>His poems and letters are a bit of behavior placed on paper and preserved. Basic profiler&#39;s principle: behavior reflects personality. Even the determination that he is lying will perhaps give an insight. If Tom&#39;s right - I&#39;m guessing it was Tom who figured it out, or got a tip - about the little word game BTK is playing with &#34;PET RAT IN A&#34;, it tells us just what kind of trickster he thinks he is. <BR> <BR>His games, the poems, and letters tell me one thing; he thinks he&#39;s funny. BTK has his own peculiar-to-him sense of humor. It is that of a very cruel and very clever child, in many ways. I&#39;ve always imagined that his little drops, his letters showing up in the press have given him a real thrill up the spine, a serious charge that probably made him jittery and hyper, almost manic-seeming. <i>&#40;That is not to insinuate he is possibly a bipolar - there is simply no way to determine that yet, if you ask me. I doubt he is - that mental illness directly affects impulse control - the mania - and for a serial, it does seem that BTK can exert some decent impulse control.&#41;</i> <BR> <BR>See, to him, as long as he remains free, I think there&#39;s an element of <i>fun</i> in all this. The poems are just one very interesting component of his fun.  <BR> <BR>If I&#39;m right, just 30% right, imagine just how sick that really is. <BR> <BR>The bottom line is, however I may personally intellectualize certain mysteries BTK presents, my heart agrees with Q, no matter what. There is no art as I understand it in this person. There is only yawning emptiness, at best. To be squashed would be a kind fate for him, in my eyes. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3743-->
<!--Post: 3744--><!--Time: 1105167519-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3744"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 10:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->That is right on the money. He is nothing more than a plagarist and a poor one at that. His attempt to &#34;fool&#34; the wpd/fbi into thinking he has a higher level of creativity and thinking is part of the game. It&#39;s also extremely narcissistic. He has no talent - he only thinks he is fooling everyone. It&#39;s a waste of time to think he is original - but it isn&#39;t a waste of time to try to figure out who he is copying and why. Old grudges? Old feuds?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3744-->
<!--Post: 3752--><!--Time: 1105176178-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3752"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 1:22 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->My wife thinks I&#39;m a Beverly Hillbilly, so I can&#39;t think of anything more fun than rating somebody&#39;s poetry.  They could even do a reality show - &#34;redneck geeks rate fancy-pants poetry&#34;. <BR> <BR>Personally, I think the first &#40;Oh!  Death to Nancy&#41; is a total juvenile rip-off - the kind we did in freshman high-school English class to relieve boredom while we were not listening to the teacher.  You can&#39;t even call it poetry, for goodness sake.  I&#39;ll blind your legs?    Which planet are we talking about here?  At least OUR little English-class peanut gallery would have done that one on purpose, and laughed all the way to the principal&#39;s office.  Even if you forgive all the errors &#40;I&#39;ve known brilliant dyslexics who write like sixth-graders&#41;, it&#39;s still just a cheap rehash that diminishes the original.  It is merely a few lines from a culturally interesting song that have been used as a framework to graft on a callous, insulting description of his murder ritual.  For cynical, evil jokedom it gets a 9, but for poetry it&#39;s a 2.  Which is no insult to BTK, because he never sent it as a poem, but merely as an evil, cynical joke. <BR> <BR>The second &#40;Oh, Anna Why Didn&#39;t You Appear&#41; is more interesting.  BTK went a bit vulnerable on this one, because I think he really tried to give us a window into his mind, which of course displays little or no conscience.  I also think BTK wanted to make it appear original and deep.  It may bear a likeness to a real poem somewhere, but I do believe that he crafted each line down to the word.  Nevertheless, in reality, if you dig past the fancy chaff, you can see that it&#39;s merely a more clever, sophisticated version of the first poem.  I can see the fakery because it&#39;s exactly how a wiseguy geek like me would fake it.  Most of it is just porno words - which overlap in a cheap and tawdry way &#40;imagine my Southern accent as I say it&#41; with the vocabulary of fancy-schmantzy literature.  Drop a couple of those words into some abstract and insightful prose and you&#39;re a local genius, but pack &#34;lofty&#34; next to &#34;bosom&#34; and you get porno.  BTK took a few pages of Playboy-level poetry, minus the limericks and the punchlines, and blended it with his MO, his bedtime female visualizations, and his treasure-fondling.  That takes more skill than the first poem, but it&#39;s still just a cheap glorification of his lethal masturbation technique.  In the end, the average is roughly the same.  He loses points on evil jokedom - I&#39;d give it a 6 - because he loses the sharp edge with all the porno words.  He picks up style points, but the only thing that makes his version of a skin-rag poem more interesting than the source is that he either kills the chick in the wind-blown grass, or wishes he could.  So what does Playboy poetry deserve?  You tell me, but I&#39;d give it a 6 for effort. <BR> <BR>BTK is capable of better, in my opinion.  Constrained by the failure to admit his wrong, he can do no better than what we see in these poems.  Frankly, I was far more moved by his first letter, complete with spelling and grammatical errors.  He showed a brief flash of desperation from the good kid who got buried under years of some kind of crap - abuse, psychological neglect, or maybe just the self-hatred that was so often felt by sexual deviants back in those days.  Growing up today, he would probably leave home, move to San Francisco, get a leather boyfriend, and write short stories for one of those local papers that you pick up outside of Starbucks.  But he would be happy, and he would have never killed all those people.  Everybody would win. <BR> <BR>BTK - if you&#39;re reading this, I want you to give it up.  Not so you can tell your &#34;story&#34; like everybody says.  THAT story will never be great, just like this poetry can&#39;t be great.  You can only tell a great story if you attempt to redeem yourself - if you throw off the crap and let the tears flow.  I know there&#39;s this little piece of you that wishes none of this ever happened.  That&#39;s the piece that I&#39;m talking to.  I&#39;m not talking to the smirking jerk that nobody likes, because that piece of you is going to die.  There are people that will say that you can&#39;t change, but they&#39;re wrong.  I know a kid who bored holes in cats and threatened to gun down his whole school - he&#39;s an angel now, and he is loved.  There are people who will tell you that it&#39;s too late, but they&#39;re wrong.  It is never too late to do the right thing.  There is at least one living victim that I am aware of who has forgiven you.  That may seem strange, but you need to trust it.  You may very well start the next phase of your life with only that single friend.  But as you open up and let out the pain and the sorrow and the regret, others will come, and you will find something good that will fill you up.  Then, and only then, will you have a great story to tell.  And I will guarantee you that people will listen.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3752-->
<!--Post: 3754--><!--Time: 1105180179-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3754"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 2:29 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->PS to Tom - sorry to crosspost a message to BTK in this thread, but it seemed like the best place to put it relative to my &#34;review&#34;.  Perhaps his vanity will lead him to it. <BR> <BR>As to the wisdom of the post, I have my flak jacket on, so y&#39;all don&#39;t be shy.  Granted, 6 seems a bit high, but I&#39;m taking 5 as the average for all people capable of writing anything.  I&#39;m really trying to be honest in spite of my feelings.  As to my message to him, please bear in mind that offering forgiveness to those who truly repent is not even close to optional in my religion, no matter how despicable the crime.  This does not mean that justice should be denied in the slightest.  Nor should it be taken as any indication that I have any illusions as to the likelihood of an SK turning himself in.  Nevertheless, BTK is not your average SK, so there may be a chance.  I&#39;m not the only person who is trying to reach out.  Thanks! -pax<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3754-->
<!--Post: 3755--><!--Time: 1105182873-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3755"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 3:14 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Q: I couldn&#39;t agree more. <BR>Steve_Huff, Lilymarie and Pax56, thank you. I understand the need for forensic analysis; I&#39;m happy that BTK is bereft of any exceptional talent. I enjoy the frank responses. <i>Finnegan&#39;s Wake</i> indeed! I was almost expecting Shakespeare comparisons. &#40;Not from anyone here but from elsewhere.&#41; Great point about BTK&#39;s absolute lack of a sense of humor. To that, I&#39;d add that his wit is minimal. He&#39;s a sick child in an adult&#39;s body. It&#39;s also interesting that he types or prints out almost all his lettrs and poems. If he&#39;d submit a poem longhand, the lettering would probably be accomplished by use of a Crayola Crayon. <BR> <BR>Poe was mentioned. I know Poe&#39;s stories almost word for word. Poe&#39;s poems, well, there are three of four I know. If BTK is looking for Poe material to plaigerize, &#34;Ulalume&#34; is my candidate although &#34;El Dorado,&#34; for it&#39;s title alone, would be more appropriate. Thanks again. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3755-->
<!--Post: 3757--><!--Time: 1105184079-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3757"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 3:34 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->annabel lee would be perfect or for annie would intrest BTK the most I think. Both poems about women Poe knew that died.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3757-->
<!--Post: 3782--><!--Time: 1105228902-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3782"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 4:01 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;Annabelle Lee&#34; would be better, oh yes. In a Poe short story sense, BTK&#39;s &#34;Descent Into The Maelstrom&#34; is in full throttle and &#34;The Oblong Box&#34; may be nigh.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3782-->
<!--Post: 3792--><!--Time: 1105236351-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3792"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 6:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ah! George! One thing I have totally missed through all of this reading is the fact that there is no sense of humor, cleverness or twist to anything he has written or said. The great writers he pretends to be in a league with are all about wit and satire. Great observation. This guy is technically smart but an idiot when it comes to nuances. I&#39;ll bet he is one of those creepy peeps you run across that doesn&#39;t pick up on other&#39;s body language or facial expressions when he is being offensive or rude. Nice.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3792-->
<!--Post: 3804--><!--Time: 1105250311-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3804"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 9:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks, Lilymarie. I see a guy who, when speaking to you, is emotionless. The sort of person whose talk is the is-he-being-serious-or-is-he-being-sarcastic variety. I sense this in the poems I&#39;ve read, as things seem a little forced and stilted in places. However, an indifferent countenance, in person, is a good way of maintaining control or giving the impression of same. To some, his aloofness may come off as that of a big shot or a confident professional. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3804-->
<!--Post: 3805--><!--Time: 1105251088-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3805"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 10:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Two or three years ago, someone sent me this poem and asked for an opinion. I had to say that I didn&#39;t understand the poem, except superficially. I remember saying that the word &#34;arrogant&#34; in the final line seemed the most interesting to me. I can analyze prose but not poetry. I don&#39;t know why poetry baffles me. Anyway, here&#39;s a link to the poem, which is considered a modern classic.&#40;?&#41; I see a lot of BTK here, although he could never write this well, of course. <a href="http://www.cityintherain.com/poems/vitalx.html" target=_top>http://www.cityintherain.com/poems/vitalx.html</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3805-->
<!--Post: 3807--><!--Time: 1105254930-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3807"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 11:15 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George, I must confess I have the same dilemma. I am much better at poetry that is prose and/or sonnet based - in other words, ripe for a screenplay or script - than abstract on it&#39;s own terms. I don&#39;t think we&#39;re missing anything - I just don&#39;t believe we have the talent to write. WHICH is the point - BTK can&#39;t admit that not only does he not &#34;get it&#34; - he can&#39;t begin to &#34;create it&#34;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3807-->
<!--Post: 3813--><!--Time: 1105259452-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3813"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 09, 2005 - 12:30 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Yes, Lily. I just finished rereading one of my favorite novels, <i>Pale Fire</i>. There is a 999 line poem with extensive commentary. Many consider this book fairly difficult to read. For me, the allusion-filled, wicked commentary is easy. But I find the poem  difficult, except for the first twelve lines, which are marvelous. <BR> <BR>The final line of &#34;Oh, Death To Nancy&#34; and the final line of the above linked poem are slightly disturbing to me. One potent reference to Spring  too. The linked poem is also imbued with implied deviant sexuality and quite possibly, violent sexual death. In fact, the linked poem is scary. BTK&#39;s poems aren&#39;t. That&#39;s the difference. Although I was reminded of BTK in the above. I&#39;ll now make way for the real poetry enthisiasts. GREAT thread, but it&#39;s over my head. Thanks for the opinions, everyone. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3813-->
<!--Post: 3997--><!--Time: 1105511253-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3997"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 11, 2005 - 10:27 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It&#39;s over my head too, George, but it is  interesting, and a lot of reading to catch up on.   <BR> <BR>The title Curly Locks seems to have the &#34;X factor&#34; when you think about how the words rhyme with Shirley&#39;s first name and Nancy&#39;s last name. Like BTK was purposely linking them with the title. Shirley/Curly and Locks/Fox.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3997-->
<!--Post: 4027--><!--Time: 1105545480-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4027"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 7:58 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Bookworm, <BR> <BR>The actual title he gave it was Shirleylocks and according to the reporting of the time he ended the letter he included it with &#34;a poem for Fox next&#34; or words to that effect. <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4027-->
<!--Post: 4051--><!--Time: 1105579941-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4051"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 5:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Ron, <BR>Thank you for the detail. Curly Locks is two separate words. Maybe BTK wanted Shirleylocks to look more like Goldilocks, which is all one word.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4051-->
<!--Post: 4053--><!--Time: 1105581592-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4053"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 5:59 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Didn&#39;t he suggest the name &#34;Poetic Strangler&#34; in the letter he sent after Shirley Vian?  Was this the letter that contained the first poem?  Or did it precede the first poem? <BR> <BR>At any rate, it suggests a certain bit of pride in his literary endeavors.  However, I just cannot fathom someone who was really knowledgeable in the field to submit poems of such small calibre, especially if he were suggesting they become his namesake. <BR> <BR>Or maybe he just has a really dark sense of humor.  I can dig that.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4053-->
<!--Post: 4061--><!--Time: 1105601933-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4061"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 11:38 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Juno, I agree. The snippets from those letters can be confusing and over the decades these letters were rarely seen, as Tom mentions. If it&#39;s true about a &#34;Poetic Stangler&#34; suggestion as a &#34;name&#34; then I&#39;d go with the idea that BTK&#39;s narcissism was such that even early on, he considered himslef a poet of considerable talent. Steve_Huff made as good an observation as I&#39;ve ever seen in his Jan 7, 10:34 post, especially paragraph four. I think it&#39;s a bullseye. So did Pax56, two posts later, especially paragraph three. &#40;Can those two <i>write</i>, or what?&#41; A really dark sense of humor works too; it&#39;s BTK&#39;s unique brew of humor and notable for its lack of same. The only thing I&#39;ve seen which ever came close to a sense of humor was the &#34;I find it unamusing...&#34; line from one of his letters. He was completely serious, and I did let go with one chuckle. <BR> <BR>Bookworm, thanks! I can&#39;t even follow the meter of most poems, let alone the content.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4061-->
<!--Post: 4062--><!--Time: 1105607065-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4062"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 13, 2005 - 1:04 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;...I&#39;ll bet he is one of those creepy peeps you run across that doesn&#39;t pick up on other&#39;s body language or facial expressions when he is being offensive or rude. Nice...&#34; <BR> <BR>I couldn&#39;t agree more with this statement, lilymarie.  <BR> <BR>George, thank you for the kind words about my observation and writing. <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s a question; have new poems accompanied any of the recent communications? Maybe the next one? If so...will he have improved? Okay, that was a tongue-in-cheek question.  <BR> <BR>George, you mentioned meter...that&#39;s the other thing about BTK poems. There is no meter. There is no &#34;inflection&#34;. They are as flat as his voice mechanically sounding from that old 911 tape.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4062-->
<!--Post: 4063--><!--Time: 1105615556-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4063"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 13, 2005 - 3:25 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, you&#39;re welcome. Few know that the Fox necklace was wrapped in paper, upon which BTK had written a poem!: <BR>My heart <BR>is <BR>like a park- <BR>ing meter and <BR>I&#39;m <BR>about to <BR>expire. <BR>  <BR>One cannot help but notice the puling, syrupy self-absorption which is seen in the &#34;Oh Anna&#34; poem. &#40;Sorry &#39;bout that.&#41; Seriously, I had been considering a question about BTK&#39;s poetry but with only two poems from which to extrapolate, I don&#39;t know. I had wondered whether or not BTK  had gone through a &#34;I&#39;m dedicated to artistic truth&#34; phase. This idea struck me when reading an old book, which mentions this from &#34;an old poet:&#34; <BR> <BR>The moral sense in mortals is the duty <BR>We have to pay on mortal sense of beauty. <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t see these lofty ideals in BTK&#39;s poems and I should be struck more often for the thought. <BR>Considering BTK&#39;s focus on images I wonder if he used movie dialogue in any poems? One line from a prescient 1950 film: &#34;..Then there was a maharaja who came all the way from India to beg one of her silk stockings. Later he strangled himself with it.&#34; <i>Sunset Boulevard</i>. <BR> <BR>&#34;Oh, Anna,&#34; it is held, may be original. Can the song &#34;Oh, Donna&#34; by Ritchie Valenz be ruled out as a source? I don&#39;t recall the words but the titles are similar. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4063-->
<!--Post: 4072--><!--Time: 1105639391-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4072"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Wade<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wade<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 13, 2005 - 10:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George- <BR>The necklace was wrapped in paper?  When?  If he took the necklace and wpd is looking for it what are you referring to??  Supposedly no one knows where the necklace is? <BR>Do you know something we don&#39;t about the necklace?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4072-->
<!--Post: 4077--><!--Time: 1105648416-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4077"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 13, 2005 - 12:33 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wade, no. My post was facetious, I was following up on what Steve said about how poor BTK&#39;s poems were and I also made a pun on the word &#34;meter.&#34; I don&#39;t know anything special about the necklace.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4077-->
<!--Post: 4079--><!--Time: 1105649154-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4079"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 13, 2005 - 12:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George - I&#39;ve nearly made similar jokes several times <i>&#40;thought yours was funny, too&#41;</i>, but I&#39;d noticed on other message boards that such things are likely to bring immediate questions such as Wade&#39;s.  <BR> <BR>Very paranoid environment to post in, but I guess it&#39;s understandable to a degree. Some people won&#39;t let up, either, and by the time all is said and done the entire board thinks the joker is BTK and they are holding torches and pitchforks and chanting, &#34;SWAB HIM, SWAB HIM...&#34; <BR> <BR>I still want to see if BTK has actually written new poetry, and if it&#39;s remotely coherent.  <BR> <BR>Another thing to think about where the poems are concerned...&#34;Oh Anna&#34; is the best example, but they all emphasize the bond between sex and death. He even says it; &#39;sexual death to you for me.&#39; Note the wording, &#34;for me&#34;. It is always about him and no one else. He doesn&#39;t just sexualize death, he romanticizes it. When did that get fused in his psyche, I wonder? I would be interested to find out if there was a rape witnessed as a child, or a murder, most likely of a domestic sort. Witnessing such things, or witnessing abuse that is similar, could be just as big a catalyst in the mind of a young sociopath.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4079-->
<!--Post: 4080--><!--Time: 1105651549-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4080"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 13, 2005 - 1:25 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks, Steve. You&#39;re right, I was punchy and lost sight of the big picture for a moment. <BR> <BR>I would also very much like to see more BTK poetry. When he romanticizes sexual death, I wonder if he&#39;s utterly sincere or if he&#39;s trying to shock. 75/25 sincere/shock I&#39;d guess. <BR> <BR>He probably had an awful childhood, but so do others, they don&#39;t end up murderers, etc.... In BTK&#39;s case, I sense also an extreme amount of psychological abuse, as if he had a set of rigid rules to remember and if he broke a rule it always meant a philippic of 110 decibels, perhaps accompanied by a physical thrashing. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4080-->
<!--Post: 4085--><!--Time: 1105656969-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4085"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tbear<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tbear<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 13, 2005 - 2:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->this may be an odd thought, and not generally accepted, but i did find this O Death poem from Anne Boleyn, King Henry VIII wife that was beheaded for treason. <BR><a href="http://www.love-poems.me.uk/boleyn_anne_o_death_rock_me_asleep.htm" target=_top>http://www.love-poems.me.uk/boleyn_anne_o_death_ro ck_me_asleep.htm</a> <BR> <BR>just one of my &#39;out there&#39; thoughts<!--/Text--></td></tr>
